    id: 16,
    title: "The New England Vampire Panic",
    fact: "In 19th-century New England, grieving families exhumed their dead relatives and burned their hearts, believing consumption (tuberculosis) was caused by the dead feeding on the living.",
    details: `Long after Europe's vampire hysteria had faded into folklore, a strikingly similar panic quietly gripped rural New England throughout the 18th and 19th centuries - not in misty Transylvanian villages, but in the farming communities of Rhode Island, Connecticut, and Vermont, where families dug up their own dead and burned their hearts in a desperate attempt to save the living.

The panic was driven by tuberculosis, then known as "consumption" for the way it seemed to consume its victims from within, wasting their bodies away over months or years. Tuberculosis is highly contagious and often spread within a single household, meaning that when one family member died of it, it was common - and medically unsurprising, by modern understanding - for another to sicken shortly after. Without any concept of bacterial infection, rural New Englanders reached for the explanation their folklore offered: that a deceased relative, not fully at rest, was returning from the grave at night to feed on the vitality of those they had left behind, draining them slowly toward death exactly as tuberculosis appeared to do.

The proposed remedy was as gruesome as the diagnosis. If a family suspected a dead relative of preying on the living, they would exhume the body - sometimes with a local physician or clergyman present to lend the procedure legitimacy - and examine it for supernatural signs, chiefly the presence of "fresh" liquid blood in the heart or organs that appeared insufficiently decomposed. In reality, decomposition rates vary enormously depending on soil conditions, temperature, and time of burial, and a body exhumed during a cold New England winter, only months after death, could easily appear far less decayed than expected, feeding the belief that something unnatural was occurring.

If the corpse was judged "undead," the remedy was to remove and burn the heart - sometimes along with the liver and lungs - reducing it to ashes. In many documented cases, those ashes were then mixed into a tonic and fed to the surviving sick relative, in the belief that consuming the burned heart would break the vampire's hold and cure the illness. The practice, while shocking by modern standards, was carried out not by outsiders accusing a family of harboring monsters, but by grieving, desperate family members themselves, trying anything to save whoever was still dying in the next room.

The most thoroughly documented case is that of Mercy Brown, a nineteen-year-old from Exeter, Rhode Island, who died of tuberculosis in 1892 - remarkably late for this practice, well within the era of germ theory and modern medicine, though rural word of that science traveled slowly. After Mercy's mother and older sister had already died of the disease, and her brother Edwin fell gravely ill as well, their father George Brown, along with the family physician, exhumed all three deceased family members. Mercy's body, having been buried during the winter in an above-ground crypt, was found with blood still present in her heart. This was taken as definitive proof. Her heart and liver were removed, burned on a nearby rock, and the ashes mixed into a medicinal drink for Edwin. He drank it - and died of tuberculosis anyway, a matter of months later.

Mercy Brown's case received sensationalized newspaper coverage at the time, and it is now widely believed to have directly influenced Bram Stoker, whose research notes and clippings for "Dracula" - published just five years later, in 1897 - include a newspaper article about the New England vampire exhumations. The folk panic of a small Rhode Island farming community may have quietly shaped the most famous vampire in literary history.

Historians and folklorists have identified dozens of similar exhumation cases across New England spanning roughly a century, concentrated heavily in Rhode Island, which earned a grim reputation as something of a regional epicenter for the practice. The custom persisted in isolated rural pockets even as urban America embraced modern medicine, a reminder of how thin the line between desperate grief, folk belief, and the search for any explanation - however dark - for the inexplicable spread of death through a single household could become.`
